"WHY DOESN'T THE GOVERNMENT JUST CALL ME DIRECTLY?"
The most common question we get. Short answer:
They aren't allowed to.
And in most cases, they don't want to.
Full explanation:
Teaming is legal
How federal contracting works
How we work with subs
Read the FAR ↗
Tap any purple tag to read that regulation on acquisition.gov, the Government's official site.
FAR 6.101 ↗ FAR 13.104 ↗
It must compete the job.
No handpicking a contractor, even on small purchases.
Read the full explanation
Federal law requires the Government to buy through competition. For larger contracts, FAR 6.101 requires full and open competition. For smaller purchases, FAR 13.104 still requires competition to the maximum extent practicable, which in practice means collecting quotes from several companies.
Giving a job to one company without competing it is called a sole-source award. It requires a written justification, signed and approved, explaining why no one else can do the work. For common trades like flooring, painting, cleaning, or moving, that justification almost never holds up, because many companies can do the work.
If a contracting officer skipped that process, every company that could have bid would have grounds to file a protest. So even if the people at a base love your work, they cannot legally pick up the phone and hand you the job.
FAR 5.101 ↗
It must post the job publicly.
Anything over $25,000 goes on SAM.gov for everyone to see.
Read the full explanation
FAR 5.101 requires agencies to publicize contract actions expected to exceed $25,000 on SAM.gov, the Government's single public posting site. Every company in the country sees the same notice at the same time, with the same deadline and the same instructions.
That means the work is not hidden from you, and it is not handed out to insiders. It is public. The catch is volume: thousands of notices post every week, each with its own attachments, amendments, and question deadlines. A prime contractor watches SAM.gov every day. A busy tradesman running jobs does not have time to, and that is exactly the gap KCAPEX fills.
FAR 4.1102 ↗
It can only award to registered companies.
No SAM.gov registration, no award, no payment.
Read the full explanation
FAR 4.1102 requires a company to be registered in SAM.gov when it submits an offer, with narrow exceptions for very small purchases. Registration is free, but it is real work:
	•	A Unique Entity ID and a CAGE code
	•	Dozens of representations and certifications, signed under penalty of federal law
	•	Banking details for electronic payment
	•	Renewal every year
After award, there is more: electronic invoicing through Government systems, wage determination compliance, limitations on subcontracting, installation access paperwork, and contract clauses that cannot be edited. A company that has not set all of this up cannot receive the award or get paid, no matter how good its work is. Anyone can register. Most tradespeople simply do not want to run that side of the business.
FAR 15.101 ↗
It buys best value.
Usually the lowest price that meets the requirement, not the most skilled tradesman.
Read the full explanation
The Government spends taxpayer money, and the contracting officer has to show the price was fair and reasonable. FAR 15.101 describes a best value continuum. At one end, the Government trades off price against quality. At the other end, it awards to the lowest price that is technically acceptable. Most facility and trade work is bought at the price-focused end.
That changes everything for a specialist. If the scope asks for acceptable epoxy flooring, being the best epoxy installer in the world earns no extra points. The acceptable installer with the better price wins.
How we work ↗
It wants one company to answer for the job.
Contracting offices are thinly staffed. One complete offer beats five trades to coordinate.
Read the full explanation
Every contract costs the Government staff time: evaluating offers, awarding, inspecting, processing modifications, paying invoices, and closing out. Contracting offices handle dozens of actions at once with small teams.
Picture a renovation that needs flooring, moving, painting, and disposal. Hiring four small companies directly means four competitions, four contracts, four invoices, and the Government coordinating the schedule between them. One prime turns that into one award, one point of contact, and one company responsible if anything goes wrong.
On a firm fixed price contract, the prime also carries the risk. If the job costs more than planned, the prime absorbs it, not the taxpayer. Contracting officers value a prime that knows the rules, submits complete paperwork, protects the scope, and never bills for work that was not authorized. That is why they would rather award to a prime than manage trades directly.
