Why doesn't the Government just hire me directly?
Because federal buyers are required to compete, publicize, and award to registered companies on value. Here is the full explanation.
Why would the government hire a small company instead of a big one?
By law, the federal government sets aside a large share of contracts for small businesses, and agencies are measured on hitting that goal. Most facility, cleaning, and trade work at government sites is bought this way. A small prime with reliable local partners is exactly what those contracting offices are looking for.
How do I get paid?
You invoice KCAPEX per the subcontract. KCAPEX invoices the Government through its electronic payment system. Federal payment is reliable but not fast, typically 30 days after acceptance, so the subcontract spells out timing up front.
Do I need to register with the government?
Not to work as our subcontractor. You need your normal business license and insurance. Your crew will need to pass a background check for access to the site, which the Government runs, and you will need to pay the required government wage rates on service work, which we build into the price.
What if the site asks me to do extra work?
Tell us the same day. Work outside the written scope is not paid unless the contracting officer approves a change. Protecting your scope is our job, and it is one of the main reasons the prime exists.
