FEDERAL CONTRACTING, EXPLAINED
Plain-language background for partners who are new to government work.
How it came to be
1953
Congress passes the Small Business Act and creates the Small Business Administration, with a mandate that a fair share of federal purchasing go to small firms.
1984
The Federal Acquisition Regulation takes effect, one rulebook every agency's contracting officer has to follow. It is why the process feels formal: it is designed to be the same for everyone.
1997
The government-wide goal for small business contracting is set in law at 23 percent of prime contract dollars, with sub-goals for specific small business categories.
2012
SAM.gov launches as the single registration and posting system. Today nearly every federal solicitation, award, and contractor is visible there to anyone, for free.
Today
Federal agencies buy hundreds of billions of dollars of goods and services a year. Facility work at bases, offices, refuges, and ranger districts is a steady, recurring piece of it, bought locally, one contract at a time.
Who is who on a contract
Contracting Officer, CO or KO. The only Government person who can sign, change, or end a contract. Handles the rules and the paperwork.
COR. Contracting Officer's Representative. The end user at the site who inspects and accepts the work. Your day-to-day customer.
Prime contractor. The company that holds the contract with the Government. That is KCAPEX.
Subcontractor. A company the prime hires to perform part or all of the work. That is you. You have a contract with the prime, not the Government.
Small business specialist. An agency employee whose job is to help small firms find and win that agency's work.
Terms you will hear
SAM.gov. The Government's registration and posting site. Free. Renewed yearly.
UEI and CAGE. A company's federal ID numbers, issued through SAM.gov. Ours: PCATNSPCZFE9 and 190W7.
NAICS code. A six-digit industry code. Solicitations are posted under a code, and size standards attach to it. 561720 is janitorial; 562910 is remediation.
Solicitation. The posted request. An RFQ asks for a quote, an RFP for a proposal, an IFB for a sealed bid.
Sources sought. A pre-solicitation notice asking who can do the work. Responding shapes how the job gets bought. No pricing yet.
Set-aside. A contract reserved for small businesses, or for a specific category of small business.
PWS or SOW. Performance Work Statement or Statement of Work. The written scope. If it is not in there, it is not in the contract.
Firm fixed price. One price for the job, regardless of what it costs to perform. Most of our work is bought this way.
Period of performance. The dates the contract covers. Options can extend it.
Wage determination. The minimum hourly wage and benefits the Government requires for service workers on the contract, set by county.
Base access. The background check and badge process every worker on a military installation goes through.
Past performance. Your record of completed government work. The single most valuable thing a new contractor can earn.
CPARS. The Government's report card on a contractor's performance, visible to other agencies.
WAWF. Wide Area Workflow, the Defense Department's electronic invoicing system. The prime uses it; the sub does not need to.
