TEAMING IS LEGAL, EXPECTED, AND IN WRITING.
Look any of these up yourself at acquisition.gov or ecfr.gov.
FAR 9.603 ↗
Contractor team arrangements are recognized by the Government.
A prime hiring subcontractors is a named, approved way to do federal work.
FAR 52.219-14 ↗ 13 CFR 125.6 ↗
The prime must perform a minimum share on small business set-asides.
50% of cost for services, 15% for general construction, 25% for specialty trades.
Similarly situated, 13 CFR 125.6 ↗
A small business sub's work counts as the prime's.
On a small business set-aside, teaming with small local trades keeps the contract compliant.
Privity, FAR 44.101 ↗
The Government contracts with the prime only.
It won't direct, pay, or negotiate with subs, by design.
Flow-down clauses
Your obligations are in a written subcontract.
Wage rates, base access, and scope, before any work starts.
Commercial vs federal, at a glance
Finding work. Commercial: referrals. Federal: posted on SAM.gov.
Picking a winner. Commercial: trust. Federal: written criteria, often price.
Contract terms. Commercial: negotiated. Federal: standard clauses that can't be edited.
Changing scope. Commercial: a phone call. Federal: a written modification only.
Wages. Commercial: market rate. Federal service work: published minimums by county.
Payment. Commercial: whenever. Federal: 30 days after acceptance under the Prompt Payment Act.
Security. Commercial: show up. Federal: background check and base badge.
Track record. Commercial: reviews. Federal: past performance every agency can see.
